{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Lift up the head, etc.</b> Even though Rashi explained that it is an expression of receiving a reckoning (Exodus 30:12), nevertheless it is mentioned in the language of שְׂאוּ, כִּי תִשָּׂא, נָשֹׂא — for from the side of number He lifts them up and bears them up with surpassing eminence above all the nations that have no particular count, like this straw, to which the owner of the threshing floor gives no tally; so the nations have no particular providence, only general providence for the survival of the species, like other beasts and wild animals. But every single man of Israel is providentially watched in particularity, and even one of them is accounted as a whole nation, as it is written (Exodus 19:21): ״וְנָפַל מִמֶּנּוּ רָב״ — even if one of them falls, it is considered as though a multitude has fallen. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, gave them a number like that of the stars, concerning which it is said (Isaiah 40:26): ״הַמּוֹצִיא בְמִסְפָּר צְבָאָם לְכֻלָּם בְּשֵׁם יִקְרָא״ — so too it says here ״בְּמִסְפַּר שֵׁמוֹת״ etc., to teach that those who turn the many to righteousness are like the stars forever and ever (Daniel 12:3), and each one has particular providence. And as for what is asked (Yoma 22b) — the verses contradict one another: it is written (Hosea 2:1): ״וְהָיָה מִסְפַּר בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, from which you hear that they have a number; and afterward it is written: ״כְּחוֹל הַיָּם אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִמַּד וְלֹא יִסָּפֵר״ — according to the plain sense this is no difficulty, for before the Holy One, blessed be He, they certainly have a number; just as the stars, which He brings out and brings in by number, so they have a number, to teach that the Holy One, blessed be He, watches over each one in particularity. And what is written ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִמַּד וְלֹא יִסָּפֵר״ — that is with respect to human beings, who certainly could not count them because of their great multitude." "<b>Every male by their polls.</b> Rashi explained it by means of half-sheqels. And many of the commentators disagreed with him and said that this count was without sheqels, and the language of Scripture proves it, for it says לְגֻלְגְּלֹתָם. And they sought to resolve why specifically the first count was by means of sheqels — because at that time they needed them for the service of the Mishkan. And this resolution is far-fetched. And it appears to me that certainly the reason for the sheqels was to save them from the evil eye, which holds sway over a counted thing — and specifically over a count that is something novel. By way of parable: when one sees a hundred gold pieces belonging to a poor man, whose presumption is that he has nothing, the evil eye holds sway over him more than when one sees a thousand gold pieces belonging to a rich man, for he is already established as a man of substance. So too Israel, when they went out of Egypt, were presumed to be few in number, for with seventy souls they went down to Egypt, and they diminished there through the affliction of evil and sorrow and the casting of the children into the Nile, and suddenly they were found at a first count to be six hundred thousand, apart from children and women — certainly at that time there was reason to fear the evil eye. Unlike the other counts, for by then they were already established as a great and mighty people. And therefore King David counted them without half-sheqels, for he certainly did not transgress the commandment of the Torah, since Scripture says ״רַק בִּדְבַר אוּרִיָּה הַחִתִּי״ (1 Kings 15:5) — i.e., only in the matter of Uriah the Hittite did he sin. Rather, the commandment of the sheqels was only for the first count, for in it there was far greater cause for concern lest they suffer harm from the evil eye — both through the natural pathway as mentioned, and from the side of punishment, for that count was close to the act of the Golden Calf, and justice demanded that many of them should fall as punishment for that deed; and when it would become known through the count that six hundred thousand remained, the matter would be a novelty and the evil eye would hold sway over them, for sin abets this. Therefore at that time they needed atonement for their souls and to save them from the evil eye — unlike in all the other counts."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The citation from Yoma 22b references the well-known Talmudic discussion about the apparent contradiction between Israel being numbered and being beyond counting."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:42.655243523Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}